{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And I had ox and ass.</b> Rashi explained: a person calls many oxen 'ox.' Since he said to him, ״כֹּה אָמַר עַבְדְּךָ יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 32:5), he called himself 'servant,' to say to him that the blessing ״הֱוֵה גְבִיר לְאַחֶיךָ״ (Genesis 27:29) — that is yours, and I am your servant. And whatever a servant acquires, his master acquires, and what advantage is it to its master if the servant has nothing in his hand? And with what shall the poor servant find favor in his master's eyes? Therefore Jacob said ״יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ (Genesis 33:11) — oxen and asses, for through this I will find favor in your eyes, and what is mine is yours. This is what he said: ״וָאֶשְׁלְחָה לְהַגִּיד לַאדֹנִי לִמְצֹא חֵן בְּעֵינֶיךָ״ (Genesis 32:6), for a rich servant finds favor in his master's eyes more than a poor servant. And therefore he called many oxen 'ox,' to say that this which I have is not mine, and many oxen are like a single ox, because the authority of others is upon me; therefore everything is reckoned in my eyes as a small thing." "Another matter: he came to diminish himself and to say — just as the blessing ״הֱוֵה גְבִיר לְאַחֶיךָ״ was not fulfilled in me, so all the other blessings were not fulfilled in me. For my father said to me ״מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם וּמִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ״ — this is neither from the heavens nor from the earth; this is the language of Rashi. And it is difficult: if so, from where did the animals grow — from the air, or were they born from nothing? Rather, this is its meaning: my father said ״וְיִתֶּן לְךָ הָאֱלֹהִים מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם וּמִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 27:28), and the vav of ״וְיִתֶּן״ indicates that I am obligated to act first by way of nature, all that I find in my power to do, to labor and to bear the effort for all my needs. And this small amount that I am able to strive for through my power and the strength of my hand — HaShem will send into it the blessing of increase and abundance, until this small amount before me breaks forth into multitude. And this is the blessing ״מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם״ — like this dew, which a man neither expects nor hopes for from the children of men (Micah 5:6), so too the addition of the blessing of abundance does not depend on the labor of his hands. And likewise the earth will add from its fine flour and its oil an abundance of plenty. Therefore ״וְיִתֶּן״ is stated with a vav, for in addition to what I shall strive for through the toil of my labor, HaShem will add to me from His blessing satiation of favor and the full blessing of HaShem — as it is written (Sifrei Re'eh 158): 'One might think even if he sits idle — Scripture says, \"בְּכֹל מִשְׁלַח יָדְךָ אֲשֶׁר תַּעֲשֶׂה\"' And so I did: I was not sitting idle, but I worked and toiled greatly, and sleep fled from my eyes these twenty years, until this paltry amount that is in my hand was attained from the fullness of both hands of toil and anguishing spirit. And this small amount, like the handful of a small man — all of it is in exchange for my labor, and this is from what is mine. But no addition of blessing did I see here. And had the blessings been fulfilled in me, that HaShem would add to me ״מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם וּמִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ״ as He said — if so, by right I ought to have had oxen and asses, male and female slaves, twice as many, doubled and redoubled a thousand times. And in proportion to the blessings, all that you see I have is reckoned as nothing but a single ox and a single ass. And I do not see in the work of my hands any increase of abundance and blessing, but only what the nature of toil requires. For ״עִם לָבָן גַּרְתִּי״ (Genesis 32:5), and I did not sojourn with him for nothing, but only for the sake of the hard labor that I toiled — and everything is as wages for my labor. But an addition of blessing there is not here, for in proportion to the blessings this is nothing but one ox." "And if you would say: the blessings were indeed fulfilled in you, and the reason you have only this small amount in your hand is that you did not toil for them except for a brief time — a year or two — and given the nature of that toil, it would not have been fitting by right for you to have so very much, unless the blessing of HaShem were present in the work of your hands. To this he said: ״עִם לָבָן גַּרְתִּי וָאֵחַר עַד עַתָּה״ (Genesis 32:5) — from the day I departed from you until now, I spent twenty years in his house, and with all the toil of such a long time, my hand did not find much, but only this small amount that is in my hand, attained through the nature of the toil of great labor. And if so, this is not ״מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם וּמִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 27:28)." "And if you would say: perhaps I did not toil for all of this except for a brief time, and it broke forth into abundance on account of the blessings — and what you took as proof from the statement ״וָאֵחַר עַד עָתָּה״ is no proof, for who is to say that you toiled and labored all those days? Perhaps some of the days you worked, and afterward you dwelt there without labor, because residing there was pleasing in your eyes — for sometimes it is hard for a person to part from good people with whom he dwells and is familiar. Therefore he explained to say ״עִם לָבָן גַּרְתִּי״ — this Laban, the deceiver and the scoundrel; you know the man and his deeds. Woe is me, for I sojourned for such a long span of time with a man of evil deeds. And why did I delay until now? It was only necessity that brought me to this point, for with all the toil of this long time, my hand attained only this small amount that is before me." "And if you would say: the reason I lingered so long is that the charm of a place is upon its inhabitants, and I was a permanent resident there; therefore the parting was difficult for me. To this he said: ״גַּרְתִּי״ — I was a stranger there and not a permanent resident. And I would sojourn in the fields and forests. And if so, the charm of a place upon its inhabitants does not apply here, and the charm of its owners does not apply here either, for Laban is his name and baseness is with him. And despite all this I lingered there such a long time, and despite all this ״וַיְהִי לִי שׁוֹר וַחֲמוֹר״ — for in proportion to the blessings, everything is reckoned as one ox, and there is no blessing here but what the nature of toil requires. And if so, no blessing was fulfilled in me at all: not the blessing ״הֱוֵה גְבִיר,״ for I am a stranger; nor the blessing of the abundance of plenty that comes from ״מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם וּמִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ,״ for all this is from the side of toil and not from the side of the increase of blessing that comes from ״מִטַּל הַשָּׁמַיִם וּמִשְׁמַנֵּי הָאָרֶץ.״ Therefore it is stated: ״וַיִּקַּח מִן הַבָּא בְיָדוֹ מִנְחָה לְעֵשָׂו״ (Genesis 32:14) — he showed him as though all that he has is not from the heavens and the earth, but only from what comes into his hand, that is, from what comes to him through his strength and the labor of his hand, and did not come to him as an addition of the abundance of HaShem's blessing. And he hinted at this matter to Esau in that he commanded his messengers to respond to the question ״לְמִי אַתָּה״ etc. — ״לְעַבְדְּךָ לְיַעֲקֹב.״ And how could they say ״לְעַבְדְּךָ לְיַעֲקֹב״ — behold, ״לַה׳ הָאָרֶץ וּמְלוֹאָהּ״ (Psalms 24:1); they should have said that these servants were given to Jacob from HaShem. Rather, precisely so that he should not understand that the servants came to him by reason of the added blessing of HaShem, but only by reason of Jacob's own toil. And despite all this — far be it to say that Jacob suspended everything on his power and the strength of his hand; rather, out of fear he uttered a falsehood from his mouth. And when he made confession before HaShem, blessed be He, he said the exact opposite of this, as will be explained in what follows on the verse ״קָטֹנְתִּי״ (Genesis 32:11)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:57.468544550Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}